-- Passage rg5-main-idea-summary-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5b757339-1e96-583a-b373-e37401eeda55', 'rg5-main-idea-summary-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Bumpy Start of the Bicycle$q$, $q$The first bicycles looked very different from the ones we ride today. Early models, built about two hundred years ago, had no pedals at all. Riders sat on a wooden frame and pushed along the ground with their feet. These machines were heavy and hard to steer.

Later, inventors added pedals to the front wheel. To go faster, some builders made the front wheel huge, sometimes as tall as a grown man. These high-wheel bikes could go quickly, but they were dangerous. A small bump in the road could send a rider flying over the handlebars.

Over time, builders solved these problems one by one. They made both wheels the same size and connected the pedals to the back wheel with a chain. They added rubber tires filled with air, which made rides much smoother. Brakes made it easier to stop safely.

These changes made bicycles safer and easier to ride. Soon, many more people could use them. Workers rode bicycles to their jobs, and families rode them for fun on weekends. The bicycle became one of the most common ways to travel.$q$, 'draft')
