-- Passage rg5-main-idea-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f0cbe8d1-b892-5515-9740-229b8473ceee', 'rg5-main-idea-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '3-5', $q$A Sport Called Jump Rope$q$, $q$Many people think of jumping rope as a playground game. But in gyms around the world, jump rope is also a serious sport with teams, coaches and contests.

In speed events, jumpers try to make as many jumps as they can in a set amount of time. Judges use counters to keep track. The best jumpers can land more than five jumps every second. Their feet barely leave the floor.

In freestyle events, jumpers perform routines set to music. They cross the rope, spin, do push-ups between turns and even flip in the air. Judges give points for difficulty and for how smoothly the moves connect.

Some of the most exciting events use two long ropes turned in opposite directions. This is often called double Dutch. Two teammates turn the ropes while one or two others jump in the middle. Everyone must keep a steady rhythm, or the ropes will tangle.

Jump rope is also great exercise. It makes the heart stronger and improves balance and timing. A rope is cheap and easy to carry, so athletes can practice almost anywhere. For many young jumpers, a simple playground game has become a lifelong sport.$q$, 'draft')
